  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 237 PM EST Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A potent and large low pressure system will track \nnorthward from interior North Carolina into western Pennsylvania on \nThanksgiving Day. We are dodging a bullet with regards to this storm \ntrack, which could have resulted in more widespread and greater \namounts of freezing rain; because high pressure will be retreating \nthere will be only marginally cold air to support wintry weather. \nGiven holiday travel, caution will be advised for slippery spots \ngiven lack of treatment in this early season. While a Winter Weather \nAdvisory has been issued from tomorrow at 4 PM through Friday at 7 \nAM, we think the impacts for this event will be on the lower side. \n\nOne mitigating factor against more widespread impact are expected \nroad temperatures primarily in the middle 30s, consistent with \nrecent warmth and sunshine along with air temperatures barely \nfalling below freezing in areas that see ice accumulations. Also, \nforecast soundings show the wedge of sub-freezing air will be too \nshallow for any other precipitation type besides freezing rain (no \nrisk of sleet/snow), and the depth of this layer favors ice \naccretion primarily above 1500 feet in most areas. Strong easterly \nwinds near the surface overnight, which are discussed in Key Message \n2, also should lead to eventual erosion of this sub-freezing layer \nin areas along and west of higher terrain. In contrast, areas upwind \nsuch as near Route 100 in central Vermont will see more persistent \ncold air result in a more likely ice accumulation. In northern New \nYork, stronger northeasterly flow in the St. Lawrence Valley will \nalso support a greater chance of sub-freezing temperatures being \nmore persistent and accordingly, some ice accretion on untreated \nsurfaces. Conditions should improve by Friday morning due to \ncombination of steady rain ending and continued warming.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Strong easterly winds continue to be of some concern, \nparticularly in areas immediately downslope of the Green Mountains \nin central and southern Vermont where a Wind Advisory is now in \neffect. The timeframe of strongest winds is tricky, but there is \nvery good consensus that the low level jet will become situated \nbelow inversion height and high resolution guidance shows a \nlocalized, favorably sharp west-east pressure gradient developing in \npressure fields towards 10 PM, concurrent with a 70 knot east-\nsoutheast wind field in the 875 to 925 millibar layer just above a \nshallow low level inversion. Cross sections show some mountain wave \nbreaking, which would be expected to extend only a short distance \nfrom our mountain peaks. Therefore, the potential for damaging winds \ncurrently looks isolated to the more immediate western slopes. This \nthreat of localized 55 MPH gusts is favored towards our southern \nareas; the orientation of the spine of the Greens relative to wind \ndirection north of Camel's Hump, along with a weakening jet, \nmakes the threat lower farther north. The strongest winds are \nalso favored after steady rain ends, which opens up mainly a 1 \nto 3 hour window for the most notable impacts spreading \nnorthward after midnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Sunday night into Monday will bring our next chance for \nany weather as a low pressure system and associated fronts will \ncross our forecast area. Minimal impacts are expected with this \nsystem as boundary layer temperatures will be mild enough that \nprecipitation type will be mainly light rain/rain showers in all but \nthe highest elevations. There could be a change over to show showers \nas the precipitation comes to an end Monday night, especially across \nthe northern higher elevation areas. After maximum temperatures on \nMonday reach the mid 40s to around 50 in the afternoon, cold air \nrushes in behind departing system and lows will dip back into the \n20s.",
